Why message padding is necessary.
How input data is divided into 512-bit blocks.
How internal registers are initialized and updated.
How logical operations such as AND, OR, NOT, and XOR are used.
How modular addition and circular left rotation transform data.
How the final 128-bit hash value is generated.
How a small change in input produces a significantly different hash value due to the avalanche effect.
10. Conclusion

The MD5 hashing algorithm demonstrates the process of converting an input message of arbitrary length into a fixed-length 128-bit message digest through a sequence of data transformation operations. The algorithm involves message padding, appending the original message length, dividing the message into 512-bit blocks, initializing four internal registers, and performing 64 operations across four rounds.

Each round uses different logical functions, modular addition, and circular left rotations to transform the input data and generate intermediate values. Finally, the four internal registers are combined to produce a 32-character hexadecimal hash value.

Through this experiment, we gain a clear understanding of the internal working of a cryptographic hashing algorithm and observe the avalanche effect, which demonstrates how even a minor change in the input produces a significantly different hash output. Although MD5 is no longer considered secure for modern cryptographic applications, studying it provides a strong foundation for understanding modern hashing algorithms such as SHA-256 and SHA-3.
